In C, the best way to return an error is by using the `errno` variable and setting it appropriately. You can also return a negative value from your function to indicate an error. Here's an example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <errno.h>

int divide(int numerator, int denominator) {
    if (denominator == 0) {
        errno = EINVAL; // Set errno to indicate an invalid argument
        return -1; // Return a negative value to indicate an error
    }
    return numerator / denominator;
}

int main() {
    int result = divide(10, 0);
    if (result < 0) {
        perror("Error occurred"); // Print the error message
    }
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, if the denominator is zero, we set `errno` to `EINVAL` (invalid argument) and return -1 to indicate an error. In the `main` function, we check if the result is negative and print the error message using `perror`. This approach allows you to handle errors effectively in your C programs.

